/** Form B — parallel 85-item 2027 blueprint pack. */
window.PE_FPE_ITEMS_B = [
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-001","form":"B","number":1,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.2 Risk Analysis","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 1.2-million-ft² cold-storage warehouse is scoring an FMEA on the electric fire pump that supplies the high-rise standpipe. Severity (S), occurrence (O), and detection (D) are each 1–10. Risk priority number RPN = S × O × D.\n\n| ID | Failure mode | S | O | D |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| F1 | Automatic transfer switch fails on loss of utility power | 9 | 3 | 4 |\n| F2 | Weekly churn test skipped for one month | 4 | 6 | 2 |\n| F3 | Controller inrush trips a shared feeder that is not supervised at the fire alarm | 10 | 2 | 8 |\n| F4 | Packing-gland drip is heavier than last year | 3 | 5 | 3 |\n\nWhich row is the highest-priority risk, and what is its RPN?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"F2, RPN 48"},{"key":"B","text":"F1, RPN 108"},{"key":"C","text":"F3, RPN 160"},{"key":"D","text":"F3, RPN 20"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §2.2 Risk Analysis — RPN is the product S × O × D, not the sum.\nF1 = 9×3×4 = 108\nF2 = 4×6×2 = 48\nF3 = 10×2×8 = 160\nF4 = 3×5×3 = 45\nHighest RPN is F3 at 160.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §2.2 Risk Analysis"],"whyWrong":{"A":"F2’s RPN is 4×6×2 = 48. Skipping one churn test is higher occurrence but low severity, so it is not the highest-priority row.","B":"F1’s RPN is 9×3×4 = 108, which is the second-highest score, not the top risk.","D":"20 is S+O+D (10+2+8). FMEA RPN multiplies the three ratings, so F3 is 160, not 20."}},"distractor_notes":"A is the second-highest RPN; C added S+O+D; D is a high-occurrence / low-severity housekeeping item."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-002","form":"B","number":2,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.6 Compartment Fires","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":"figures/A/FPE-2027-A-002.svg","stem":"A 18 m airport concourse void is being analyzed with a two-zone (two-layer) model. The design fire is a 2.5 MW steady upholstered-seat fire on the lobby floor (see figure). Makeup air is required. Which modeling choice most invalidates the two-layer idealization?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Supplying makeup air as a high-velocity downward jet from a roof monitor aimed into the atrium void"},{"key":"B","text":"Using a constant radiative fraction of 0.30 for the design fire"},{"key":"C","text":"Neglecting conduction through the atrium glass when estimating layer temperature for a 10-minute detection-and-notification window"},{"key":"D","text":"Sizing mechanical exhaust from plume mass flow evaluated at the smoke-layer interface"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.6 Compartment Fires (two-layer idealization) and HB §7.1 Smoke Control Fundamentals.\nA two-zone model assumes a hot upper layer and a cooler lower layer with limited mixing.\nA downward high-velocity makeup jet driven into the void mixes the atrium and destroys the layer interface, so the two-layer state variables are no longer meaningful.\nA constant radiative fraction, plume-based exhaust, and short-time glass losses are ordinary simplifications; they do not, by themselves, mix the layers.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §3.6 Compartment Fires","HB §7.1 Fundamentals"],"whyWrong":{"B":"A constant radiative fraction is a normal two-zone input. It changes energy split, but it does not mix the upper and lower layers.","C":"Omitting glass conduction on a 10-minute detection window is a second-order heat-loss approximation. It does not destroy the two-layer idealization.","D":"Sizing exhaust from plume mass flow at the smoke-layer interface is the usual two-zone exhaust approach; it assumes the layers still exist."}},"distractor_notes":"A is a standard input; C is the usual exhaust-sizing approach; D is a second-order heat-loss omission on a short time scale."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-003","form":"B","number":3,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.1 General Fire Safety","code":"NFPA 855-2023 §4.4 / §4.9 / §9.6.5.6","edition_lock":"NFPA 855-2023","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A dedicated indoor lithium-ion battery energy storage room is proposed inside a mixed-occupancy tire-warehouse fire area. Aggregated rated energy is 2.4 MWh. No UL 9540A installation-level test report is available.\n\nThe following NFPA 855-2023 criteria are provided for this item:\n\n• Maximum stored energy in a single indoor control area for lithium-ion ESS, unless located in a dedicated-use ESS building or otherwise permitted: 600 kWh.\n• Where explosion control is required and large-scale fire testing has not demonstrated that flammable gases remain below 25% of the LFL, provide explosion control by NFPA 68, NFPA 69, or an approved performance-based design.\n• Water-based suppression shall be designed from fire-and-explosion testing; where testing is not available and groups exceed 50 kWh, an engineering analysis is required. Ordinary-hazard occupancy tables in NFPA 13-2022 are not, by themselves, a lithium-ion ESS density.\n\nWhich compliance path is consistent with NFPA 855-2023?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Keep 2.4 MWh in one fire area and protect with NFPA 13-2022 Light Hazard (0.10 gpm/ft² over 1,500 ft²)"},{"key":"B","text":"Classify the room as an outdoor installation because it has a roll-up door to the service yard"},{"key":"C","text":"Subdivide into control areas of not more than 600 kWh each with the required fire-resistive separation, provide water-based suppression supported by an engineering analysis, and provide explosion control (NFPA 68, NFPA 69, or approved performance-based design)"},{"key":"D","text":"Install a total-flooding carbon dioxide system as the primary protection and omit sprinklers"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 855-2023 (edition lock). 2.4 MWh in one indoor control area of a mixed-occupancy building exceeds the 600 kWh cap given in the stem, so subdivision (or a dedicated-use building path) is required.\nNo UL 9540A installation-level report ⇒ explosion control is still required, and suppression density cannot be taken from Light Hazard tables.\nNFPA 12 CO₂ is not a 2027 listed exam standard and is not a substitute for the 855 water-based + explosion-control path.\nA door to a yard does not convert an indoor room into an outdoor ESS installation.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 855-2023 §4.4","NFPA 855-2023 §4.9","NFPA 855-2023 §9.6.5.6 Explosion Control"],"whyWrong":{"A":"0.10 gpm/ft² over 1,500 ft² is an NFPA 13-2022 Light Hazard occupancy density. The stem states that those tables are not, by themselves, a lithium-ion ESS density, and 2.4 MWh in one mixed-occupancy control area exceeds the 600 kWh cap.","B":"A roll-up door to a yard does not convert an indoor room in a mixed-occupancy building into an outdoor ESS installation under NFPA 855-2023.","D":"Carbon dioxide total flooding keys to NFPA 12, which is not on the April 2027 listed-standard sheet. NFPA 855-2023 still requires water-based cooling (or tested equivalent) plus explosion control when UL 9540A does not clear the gas hazard."}},"distractor_notes":"A uses an NFPA 13 occupancy-hazard table as if it were an ESS density; C keys to retired NFPA 12; D misclassifies an indoor room."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-004","form":"B","number":4,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.5 Flammable and Combustible Liquids Fires","code":"NFPA 30-2024 §4.3 / §6.4.1.3","edition_lock":"NFPA 30-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A process uses a liquid with closed-cup flash point 110 °F (43 °C) and boiling point 250 °F (121 °C). The liquid is stored at 70 °F. It is also pumped through a heater that raises it to 140 °F before a reactor.\n\nNFPA 30-2024 (given for this item):\n\n• Class is a property of the liquid from closed-cup flash point and, for Class I only, boiling point. Storage temperature and process temperature do not rewrite that class.\n• Class I: flash point < 100 °F, with IA / IB / IC by flash point and boiling point\n• Class II: 100 °F ≤ flash point < 140 °F\n• Class IIIA: 140 °F ≤ flash point < 200 °F\n• Class IIIB: flash point ≥ 200 °F\n• §6.4.1.3: Storage, processing, handling, and use of Class II and Class III liquids heated at or above their flash points shall follow the requirements for Class I liquids, unless an engineering evaluation conducted in accordance with Chapter 6 justifies following the requirements for some other liquid class. That rule changes which safeguards apply to the operation; it does not reclassify the liquid.\n\nWhich statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"The liquid is Class IC because the boiling point is above 100 °F"},{"key":"B","text":"The liquid remains Class II, so the heated process may be designed solely to Class II rules even though it is at or above the flash point"},{"key":"C","text":"The liquid is Class II based on flash point. Storage at 70 °F remains under Class II requirements. The 140 °F process shall follow Class I requirements unless a Chapter 6 engineering evaluation justifies otherwise"},{"key":"D","text":"Heating to 140 °F reclassifies the liquid as Class I, and boiling point 250 °F makes the heated process Class IIIB"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 30-2024 §4.3: class is from flash point (and boiling point only inside Class I). FP = 110 °F ⇒ the liquid is Class II at 70 °F, at 140 °F, and in the drum. IA/IB/IC do not apply (FP is not < 100 °F). BP 250 °F does not make it IIIB.\n§6.4.1.3 does not rewrite the class. Storage at 70 °F is below FP, so Class II requirements apply to that storage. The 140 °F process is at/above FP, so that operation shall follow Class I requirements unless a Chapter 6 engineering evaluation justifies otherwise.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 30-2024 §4.3","NFPA 30-2024 §6.4.1.3","HB §3.5 Flammable and Combustible Liquids Fires"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Class IC exists only inside Class I (flash point < 100 °F). This liquid’s flash point is 110 °F, so it is Class II. Boiling point is not the Class II vs IC discriminator, and 70 °F storage temperature does not assign the class.","B":"The liquid does remain Class II — that part is right — but “still Class II” is not “still design only to Class II.” §6.4.1.3 requires the 140 °F process to follow Class I requirements unless a Chapter 6 evaluation says otherwise.","D":"Heating does not reclassify the liquid. It stays Class II. Class IIIB requires flash point ≥ 200 °F; boiling point 250 °F is irrelevant to that. §6.4.1.3 changes which requirements apply to the heated operation, not the name of the class."}},"distractor_notes":"B uses the IA/IB/IC branch on a liquid whose FP is ≥ 100 °F; C treats heating or boiling point as a reclassification; D treats unchanged class as unchanged safeguards."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-005","form":"B","number":5,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §6.2 Heat Detector RTI","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An engineer wants to estimate fixed-temperature heat-detector actuation time in a 3.0 m high middle-school classroom wing. The design fire is a fast t² fire under a smooth, unobstructed ceiling. A two-zone compartment model is already built for smoke-layer filling. Which statement about using that model for detector actuation is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Zone models are prohibited for any detector question; only LES CFD is acceptable"},{"key":"B","text":"The two-zone model is the preferred tool for ceiling-jet detector actuation because it resolves ceiling-jet velocity and temperature as a function of radius from the plume"},{"key":"C","text":"Ceiling-jet correlations (or an algebraic detector-response model using RTI) are required; a two-layer zone model does not resolve the ceiling jet that bathes the detector"},{"key":"D","text":"Increasing the radiative fraction from 0.30 to 0.35 will always predict earlier heat-detector actuation in a zone model"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §6.2 Heat Detector RTI and HB §3.6 Compartment Fires.\nDetector actuation is driven by the ceiling jet (local gas temperature and velocity at the device), which a two-layer zone model does not spatially resolve.\nUse the handbook ceiling-jet / RTI (or equivalent algebraic) method. Raising the radiative fraction actually reduces the convective fire plume strength, which would typically delay a heat detector. LES is not the only acceptable tool.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §6.2 Heat Detector RTI","HB §3.6 Compartment Fires"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Handbook ceiling-jet / RTI (algebraic) methods are the usual tool. Large-eddy CFD is not required for a detector-actuation estimate.","B":"A two-zone model stores one upper-layer temperature. It does not resolve ceiling-jet velocity or temperature versus radius, which is what actually bathes a spot-type heat detector.","D":"Raising the radiative fraction reduces the convective fire plume. A heat detector in the ceiling jet would typically actuate later, not earlier."}},"distractor_notes":"A attributes ceiling-jet resolution to a zone model; C has the radiative-fraction effect backwards for a heat detector; D overstates the tool requirement."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-006","form":"B","number":6,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.2 Risk Analysis","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A paint-mix room has three independent layers of protection. Probability of a spill is 0.04 per year. Given a spill, ignition probability is 0.20. Given ignition, the probability that the deluge system fails is 0.05.\n\nExpected loss:\n• Ignition and deluge failure: $2,000,000\n• Ignition and deluge success: $50,000\n• Spill with no ignition: $10,000\n\nWhat is the expected annual loss from this scenario?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"$16,000 / yr"},{"key":"B","text":"$1,180 / yr"},{"key":"C","text":"$1,500 / yr"},{"key":"D","text":"$800 / yr"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §2.2 Risk Analysis — expected loss is the sum of (probability × consequence) over mutually exclusive branches.\nP(spill ∩ ign ∩ fail) = 0.04 × 0.20 × 0.05 = 0.0004 → $800\nP(spill ∩ ign ∩ success) = 0.04 × 0.20 × 0.95 = 0.0076 → $380\nP(spill ∩ no ign) = 0.04 × 0.80 = 0.032 → $320\nEAL = 800 + 380 + 320 = $1,500.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §2.2 Risk Analysis"],"whyWrong":{"A":"$16,000 is 0.04 × 0.20 × $2,000,000, which ignores both deluge success and unignited spills and treats every ignition as a total loss.","B":"$1,180 is the two ignited branches only ($800 + $380). It drops the unignited-spill branch (0.032 × $10,000 = $320).","D":"$800 is only the deluge-failure branch. You still have to add deluge-success losses and unignited-spill losses."}},"distractor_notes":"B omits unignited spills; C is only the fail branch; D ignores deluge success."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-007","form":"B","number":7,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §2.1 General Fire Safety","code":"NFPA 855-2023","edition_lock":"NFPA 855-2023","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Which assignment correctly describes the role of each document for a stationary lithium-ion energy storage installation?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"NFPA 855-2023 is only a product listing; UL 9540A replaces NFPA 13 and NFPA 855 for indoor rooms; occupancy-hazard tables are required for every BESS"},{"key":"B","text":"UL 9540 = product listing of the ESS equipment; UL 9540A = large-scale thermal-runaway fire-propagation test data; NFPA 855-2023 = installation standard (siting, stored energy, suppression, detection, explosion control); NFPA 13-2022 occupancy-hazard tables alone do not set a lithium-ion ESS sprinkler density"},{"key":"C","text":"UL 9540 = installation standard; UL 9540A = product listing; NFPA 855-2023 = optional commentary; NFPA 13-2022 occupancy-hazard tables set the ESS density"},{"key":"D","text":"UL 9540A = product listing; UL 9540 = fire-propagation test method; NFPA 13-2022 Extra Hazard Group 2 is the default lithium-ion ESS density; NFPA 855-2023 applies only outdoors"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 855-2023 distinguishes product listing (UL 9540), large-scale fire-and-explosion testing (UL 9540A), and the installation standard (855 itself).\nNFPA 13-2022 occupancy-hazard densities (Light / Ordinary / Extra) are not a lithium-ion ESS design density without fire-and-explosion testing or an engineering analysis under 855.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 855-2023","HB §2.1 General Fire Safety"],"whyWrong":{"A":"NFPA 855-2023 is the installation standard, not a product listing. UL 9540A supports 855; it does not replace 855 or 13 occupancy tables as a blanket indoor method.","C":"This swaps UL 9540 (listing) with NFPA 855 (installation) and treats 13 occupancy-hazard tables as an ESS density. 9540A is the test method, not the product listing.","D":"UL 9540 is the listing; 9540A is the test. Extra Hazard Group 2 is not a default lithium-ion ESS density. NFPA 855-2023 covers indoor and outdoor installations."}},"distractor_notes":"B/C swap listing vs test vs installation; D treats 855 as a listing and 9540A as a replacement code."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-008","form":"B","number":8,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.4 Fire Protection Analysis—Information Sources","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A warehouse 80 ft × 200 ft × 32 ft high is currently protected with ESFR sprinklers listed for the existing Class III commodity. The owner wants to change to cartoned unexpanded plastic. Which analysis is the most appropriate first screening tool for sprinkler adequacy?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"An FDS model of the entire 16,000 ft² building at a 2 in. grid as the only acceptable method"},{"key":"B","text":"NFPA 13-2022 storage-commodity design criteria and the ESFR sprinkler listing limitations"},{"key":"C","text":"A two-zone atrium smoke-fill model of the entire warehouse volume"},{"key":"D","text":"A hand calculation of ultrafast t² time to 20 MW with no sprinkler interaction, used as the acceptance criterion"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §2.4 Information Sources — listed water-based storage protection is scored against NFPA 13-2022 and the sprinkler listing, not against a smoke-filling model.\nCartoned unexpanded plastic is a commodity-class change. Check whether the existing ESFR K-factor, temperature rating, spacing, and storage height remain inside NFPA 13-2022 Chapter 20+ design tables and the manufacturer listing.\nA zone atrium model does not evaluate sprinkler-commodity pairing. A free-burn t² fire ignores suppression. Full-building 2 in. LES is neither required nor a first screening tool.","miss_type":"handbook-search","repair_loci":["HB §2.4 Fire Protection Analysis—Information Sources","NFPA 13-2022 storage commodity chapters"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Full-building 2 in. FDS is not a required first screening tool and is not the listed method for commodity-class changes.","C":"A two-zone atrium smoke-fill model estimates layer height and temperature. It does not check whether the existing ESFR listing still covers cartoned unexpanded plastic.","D":"A free-burn ultrafast t² time to 20 MW ignores sprinkler operation. Storage protection is scored against NFPA 13-2022 commodity/ESFR criteria, not an unsuppressed fire-size clock."}},"distractor_notes":"A applies a smoke-control tool to a storage-sprinkler question; C ignores suppression; D treats CFD as mandatory first-pass design."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-009","form":"B","number":9,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.A","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Steady one-dimensional conduction through a gypsum partition. Thermal conductivity k = 0.17 W/m·K (given). Thickness L = 16 mm. Exposed area A = 12 m². Surface temperatures are 120 °C on the fire side and 40 °C on the unexposed face.\n\nFourier’s law as used in the handbook: q = k A ΔT / L.\n\nWhat is the conductive heat-transfer rate, to the nearest 0.1 kW?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"10.2 kW"},{"key":"B","text":"10,200 kW"},{"key":"C","text":"15.3 kW"},{"key":"D","text":"0.010 kW"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer, Fourier’s law (stated in the stem).\nL = 0.016 m, ΔT = 80 K\nq = (0.17 W/m·K)(12 m²)(80 K) / (0.016 m) = 163.2 / 0.016 = 10,200 W = 10.2 kW.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.3 Heat Transfer"],"whyWrong":{"B":"10,200 W is the correct wattage, but the stem asks for kW. Dividing by 1,000 gives 10.2 kW, not 10,200 kW.","C":"15.3 kW uses ΔT = 120 °C instead of 120 − 40 = 80 K. Both surface temperatures are given; Fourier’s law uses the difference.","D":"0.010 kW is the result of leaving thickness as 16 (mm) instead of converting to 0.016 m, which is off by a factor of 1,000 the other way."}},"distractor_notes":"B reports watts as kW; C leaves L in mm; D forgets the unexposed-face temperature."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-010","form":"B","number":10,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A blackbody target views a large fire that may be treated as a black surface at 1,400 °F. Surroundings are 70 °F. Net radiative heat flux is\n\nq″ = σ (T₁⁴ − T₂⁴)\n\nwith σ = 0.1714 × 10⁻⁸ Btu/h·ft²·R⁴ (given). Temperatures in Rankine: T(R) = T(°F) + 460.\n\nWhat is q″, in Btu/h·ft², to the nearest 100?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"6,600 Btu/h·ft²"},{"key":"B","text":"1,140 Btu/h·ft²"},{"key":"C","text":"20,400 Btu/h·ft²"},{"key":"D","text":"20,500 Btu/h·ft²"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer, Stefan–Boltzmann (σ given in the stem).\nT₁ = 1,400 + 460 = 1,860 R\nT₂ = 70 + 460 = 530 R\nT₁⁴ = 1.19688 × 10¹³\nT₂⁴ = 7.890 × 10¹⁰\nΔT⁴ = 1.1890 × 10¹³\nq″ = (1.714 × 10⁻⁹)(1.1890 × 10¹³) = 20,380 Btu/h·ft² → 20,400 to the nearest 100.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.3 Heat Transfer"],"whyWrong":{"A":"6,600 Btu/h·ft² is the result of raising 1,400 °F to the fourth power without converting to Rankine (1,860 R). The stem requires T(R) = T(°F) + 460.","B":"1,140 Btu/h·ft² is the magnitude you get if SI σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ is mixed with Fahrenheit temperatures. Use the US σ given in the stem.","D":"20,500 Btu/h·ft² is σ T₁⁴ with T₂ dropped. Net flux subtracts the surroundings term T₂⁴, which changes the rounded hundreds place from 20,500 to 20,400."}},"distractor_notes":"B skips Rankine conversion; C drops T₂; D mixes SI σ with US temperatures."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-011","form":"B","number":11,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A design fire is specified as a fast t² fire, Q = α t², with growth coefficient α = 0.0469 kW/s² (given). Time t is in seconds and Q is in kW.\n\nAt what time does the fire reach 1.05 MW (1,050 kW)?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"150 s"},{"key":"B","text":"300 s"},{"key":"C","text":"5 s"},{"key":"D","text":"75 s"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates, t² relation (α given in the stem).\nt = √(Q / α) = √(1,050 / 0.0469) = √22,388 = 149.6 s → 150 s.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates"],"whyWrong":{"B":"300 s is approximately √(1,050 / 0.0117), the medium growth coefficient, not the fast α = 0.0469 kW/s² given in the stem.","C":"5 s is √(1.05 / 0.0469), which treats 1.05 MW as 1.05 kW. Convert 1.05 MW to 1,050 kW before substituting.","D":"75 s is approximately √(1,050 / 0.1876), the ultrafast coefficient. The stem specifies fast growth."}},"distractor_notes":"B uses medium α; C uses ultrafast α; D forgets MW to kW."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-012","form":"B","number":12,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A heptane pool fire has surface area 2.0 m². Mass burning flux is 0.055 kg/m²·s (given). Effective heat of combustion ΔH_c = 44.6 MJ/kg (given). Combustion efficiency χ = 0.85 (given).\n\nHeat release rate Q = χ ṁ ΔH_c, with ṁ = (mass flux) × (area).\n\nWhat is Q, reported to the nearest 0.1 MW?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"0.085 MW"},{"key":"B","text":"4.2 MW"},{"key":"C","text":"4.9 MW"},{"key":"D","text":"1.1 MW"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates (and HB §11.3 fuel properties as a lookup locus; values are in the stem).\nṁ = 0.055 kg/m²·s × 2.0 m² = 0.110 kg/s\nQ = 0.85 × 0.110 kg/s × 44.6 MJ/kg = 4.170 MW → 4.2 MW.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","HB §11.3 Physical and Combustion Properties of Selected Fuels"],"whyWrong":{"A":"0.085 MW is ṁ × χ with ΔH_c omitted (0.110 × 0.85). Heat of combustion is in the stem and must be included.","C":"4.9 MW is ṁ ΔH_c without the combustion efficiency χ = 0.85. The stem requires Q = χ ṁ ΔH_c.","D":"1.1 MW is the mass-loss rate 0.110 kg/s treated as if it were already MW-scale. You still have to multiply by ΔH_c and χ."}},"distractor_notes":"B omits χ (4.9 MW); C treats MJ as if they were already MW-scale without the mass flux; D is ṁ × χ with no ΔH_c."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-013","form":"B","number":13,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.A","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Hot gas at 350 °C flows over the exterior of a steel duct. Ambient air is 20 °C. Exposed area A = 4.0 m². Convective coefficient h = 25 W/m²·K (given).\n\nNewton’s law of cooling: q = h A (T_gas − T_∞).\n\nConvective heat loss, in kW, to the nearest 1 kW?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"8 kW"},{"key":"B","text":"33 kW"},{"key":"C","text":"83 kW"},{"key":"D","text":"35 kW"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer, convection (h given in the stem).\nΔT = 350 − 20 = 330 K\nq = (25 W/m²·K)(4.0 m²)(330 K) = 33,000 W = 33 kW.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.3 Heat Transfer"],"whyWrong":{"A":"8 kW is an order-of-magnitude / arithmetic slip (for example, using 80 K instead of 330 K, or dropping a factor of about 4).","C":"83 kW is 25 × 4 × ~830 W, which adds 460 as if converting to Rankine and then uses that as ΔT. In SI the kelvin difference equals the Celsius difference: 350 − 20 = 330 K.","D":"35 kW uses T_gas = 350 °C as ΔT and forgets to subtract T_∞ = 20 °C. Newton’s law uses (T_gas − T_∞) = 330 K."}},"distractor_notes":"C uses 350 °C as ΔT (forgets T_∞). D adds 460 as if converting to Rankine and then uses that as ΔT: 25×4×830 W ≈ 83 kW. B is an order-of-magnitude slip."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-014","form":"B","number":14,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.C","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.6 Compartment Fires","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Estimate flashover using the Thomas correlation provided in the stem (handbook compartment-fire family):\n\nQ_FO = 7.8 A_T + 378 A_o √H_o    (Q_FO in kW; areas in m²; H_o in m)\n\nwhere A_T is the total internal surface area of the compartment (walls + ceiling + floor, door opening deducted) and A_o and H_o are the ventilation-opening area and height.\n\nCompartment: 8.0 m × 6.0 m × 3.0 m high. One door 2.0 m wide × 2.0 m high. No other openings.\n\nPredicted flashover heat-release rate, to the nearest 0.1 MW?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"3.5 MW"},{"key":"B","text":"4.4 MW"},{"key":"C","text":"1.4 MW"},{"key":"D","text":"2.1 MW"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.6 Compartment Fires; Thomas correlation given in the stem.\nA_floor = A_ceiling = 48 m²\nA_walls = 2 × (8 + 6) × 3 = 84 m²\nGross envelope = 180 m²; deduct door 4.0 m² → A_T = 176 m²\nA_o = 2.0 × 2.0 = 4.0 m²; H_o = 2.0 m; √H_o = 1.414\nQ_FO = 7.8 × 176 + 378 × 4.0 × 1.414 = 1,373 + 2,138 = 3,511 kW = 3.5 MW.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.6 Compartment Fires"],"whyWrong":{"B":"4.4 MW uses H_o = 2 m in place of √H_o in the opening term (378 × 4 × 2 = 3,024 kW, plus 1,373 kW ≈ 4.4 MW). The correlation takes the square root of opening height.","C":"1.4 MW is only the 7.8 A_T term (7.8 × 176 ≈ 1,373 kW). The opening term 378 A_o √H_o still has to be added.","D":"2.1 MW is only the opening term (378 × 4 × √2 ≈ 2,138 kW). The wall/ceiling/floor term still has to be added."}},"distractor_notes":"B is the A_T term only (~1.4 MW); C is the opening term only (~2.1 MW); D uses H_o instead of √H_o in the opening term (≈ 4.4 MW)."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-015","form":"B","number":15,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A storage-room fire is specified as a medium t² fire until sprinklers operate, then a constant 1.2 MW until burnout. Fuel load is 180 MJ/m² over 40 m². Medium growth coefficient α = 0.0117 kW/s² (given). Fast growth coefficient α = 0.0469 kW/s² (given).\n\nWhich statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Time to 1 MW for a fast t² fire is about 50 s, and 180 MJ/m² equals the heat-release rate in MW"},{"key":"B","text":"Time to 1 MW for the medium t² fire is about 292 s, and 7.2 GJ at 1.2 MW would burn about 1.7 h"},{"key":"C","text":"Time to 1 MW for the medium t² fire is about 292 s, and the fuel load 180 MJ/m² is the heat-release rate"},{"key":"D","text":"Time to 1 MW for the medium t² fire is about 75 s, and 7.2 GJ at 1.2 MW would burn about 1.7 h"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates.\nMedium: t = √(1,000 / 0.0117) = √85,470 = 292 s.\nEnergy = 180 MJ/m² × 40 m² = 7,200 MJ = 7.2 GJ. Duration at 1.2 MW = 7,200 / 1.2 = 6,000 s = 100 min ≈ 1.7 h.\nFast t² to 1 MW is √(1,000 / 0.0469) = 146 s, not 50 s. Fuel load (MJ/m²) is energy per area, not HRR (MW).","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Fast α = 0.0469 kW/s² reaches 1 MW in about 146 s, not 50 s. Fuel load in MJ/m² is energy per area; it is not numerically the heat-release rate in MW.","C":"292 s is the correct medium time to 1 MW, but 180 MJ/m² is a fuel load (energy/area), not an HRR. HRR is power (MW).","D":"75 s is the ultrafast time-to-1-MW ballpark, not medium (α = 0.0117 kW/s² → 292 s). The 1.7 h burnout figure is correct, but the growth time is not."}},"distractor_notes":"B uses 50 s and confuses MJ/m² with MW; C uses the ultrafast time; D treats fuel load as HRR."},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-016","form":"B","number":16,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §2.1 General Fire Safety","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A performance-based atrium design uses “occupants have 10 minutes of tenability” as the sole stated acceptance criterion. No design fire, no ASET calculation, and no comparison to RSET are provided. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"A 10-minute clock is not a complete performance demonstration; acceptance requires a stated design fire, an ASET from that fire (and any smoke-control), and RSET < ASET for the required scenarios"},{"key":"B","text":"Any NFPA 13-2022 occupancy-hazard density automatically satisfies atrium performance"},{"key":"C","text":"Performance-based design allows listed codes to be ignored for egress and suppression"},{"key":"D","text":"Tenability time is a structural fire-resistance rating and is scored from ASTM E119 alone"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §2.1 / performance process, and NFPA 101-2024 performance concepts. A duration without a fire size, smoke-layer or tenability model, and an egress timeline is not a closed acceptance argument.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §2.1 General Fire Safety","NFPA 101-2024 performance"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Sprinkler density is a prescriptive suppression criterion. It does not, by itself, prove 10 minutes of tenability in an atrium.","C":"PBD still has to demonstrate the stated objectives. It is not a waiver of listed-code intent.","D":"Tenability is an occupant-exposure criterion (visibility, temperature, toxicity), not an ASTM E119 hourly rating."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-017","form":"B","number":17,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.6 Compartment Fires","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 40 m airport concourse is modeled in CFD with a uniform 2.0 m grid. The design fire is a 5 MW sofa fire on the lobby floor. Which modeling limitation is the most important?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Any CFD grid is acceptable if the domain includes the whole building"},{"key":"B","text":"NFPA 92-2024 prohibits CFD; only algebraic plume formulas may be used"},{"key":"C","text":"A 2 m cell size cannot resolve the fire plume or ceiling jet; those length scales are much smaller than 2 m, so temperature and detector/sprinkler inputs from this grid are not credible"},{"key":"D","text":"5 MW is too small to produce a plume in an atrium, so no model is required"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §3.6 and HB §3.4 — plume and ceiling-jet length scales are on the order of the fire diameter and meters of ceiling-jet depth, not 2 m cells. Grid resolution is a first-order model limitation.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §3.6 Compartment Fires","HB §3.4 Plumes and Flames"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Domain size does not fix an unresolved plume. Cell size still has to match the physics you claim to predict.","B":"NFPA 92-2024 does not ban CFD. Algebraic methods are one acceptable family, not the only one.","D":"5 MW is a credible design fire; the issue is spatial resolution, not fire size."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-018","form":"B","number":18,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.1 General Fire Safety","code":"NFPA 855-2023 §9.6.5.6","edition_lock":"NFPA 855-2023","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An outdoor walk-in lithium-ion ESS container (occupiable for maintenance) has 1.2 MWh aggregated energy. No UL 9540A installation-level report is provided. The following NFPA 855-2023 criterion is given: where explosion control is required and testing has not shown flammable gases remain below 25% of the LFL, provide explosion control (NFPA 68, NFPA 69, or approved performance-based design) for indoor rooms and occupiable walk-in units.\n\nWhich path is consistent with NFPA 855-2023?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Protect to NFPA 13-2022 Light Hazard only"},{"key":"B","text":"Use a total-flooding carbon dioxide system as the only protection, keyed to NFPA 12"},{"key":"C","text":"Treat the walk-in container as an occupiable volume and provide explosion control (68, 69, or approved PBD) plus water-based suppression supported by testing or an engineering analysis"},{"key":"D","text":"Label it outdoor and omit explosion control and suppression because it is not inside a building"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 855-2023: occupiable walk-in ESS units are not excused from explosion control just because they sit in a yard. No 9540A gas-clearance ⇒ explosion control still applies. NFPA 12 is not a 2027 listed exam standard.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 855-2023 §9.6.5.6","NFPA 855-2023 §4.9"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Light Hazard is not a lithium-ion ESS density.","B":"NFPA 12 is not on the April 2027 listed-standard sheet and is not a substitute for 855 water-based cooling plus explosion control.","D":"Outdoor siting does not wipe explosion control for a walk-in occupiable unit when testing has not cleared the flammable-gas hazard."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-019","form":"B","number":19,"domain":1,"subdomain":"1.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"analyze","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A performance-based life-safety analysis for a two-story school gymnasium must include a fire that blocks the main entrance. Which analysis is consistent with that scenario family?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Ignore blocked-exit scenarios because prescriptive travel-distance tables already include redundancy"},{"key":"B","text":"Use only a 5 MW steady fire at the geometric center of the floor, regardless of exit layout"},{"key":"C","text":"Score the problem to IBC 1006; IBC is the supplied exam standard"},{"key":"D","text":"Place a design fire that makes the main entrance path untenable and show that the remaining exits still provide RSET < ASET for the occupant load"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"HB §10.2 and NFPA 101-2024 performance: required scenarios include fires that compromise a primary egress path. Remaining capacity must still clear occupants before tenability is lost.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement","NFPA 101-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Prescriptive tables do not replace a blocked-exit performance scenario when the design is performance-based.","B":"A center-floor fire may not challenge the main entrance. Scenario selection has to match the failure being demonstrated.","C":"IBC is not a supplied 2027 exam standard. Scoring lands on NFPA 101-2024 or the handbook."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-020","form":"B","number":20,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.A","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A blackbody target sees a large fire treated as a black surface at 800 °C. Surroundings are 20 °C. σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ W/m²·K⁴ (given). T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n\nNet radiative flux q″ = σ (T₁⁴ − T₂⁴), to the nearest 1 kW/m²?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"23 kW/m²"},{"key":"B","text":"80 kW/m²"},{"key":"C","text":"75 kW/m²"},{"key":"D","text":"0.075 kW/m²"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"T₁ = 1073 K, T₂ = 293 K.\nT₁⁴ ≈ 1.326×10¹², T₂⁴ ≈ 7.4×10⁹.\nq″ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ × 1.318×10¹² ≈ 74,700 W/m² → 75 kW/m².","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.3 Heat Transfer"],"whyWrong":{"A":"23 kW/m² uses 800 K instead of 1073 K (forgot to add 273).","B":"80 kW/m² is a rounded σ T₁⁴ without a clean T₂ subtraction / rounding slip.","D":"0.075 kW/m² is 75 W/m² — off by 1,000 on the watt-to-kW conversion."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-021","form":"B","number":21,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A fuel package burns at 0.20 lb/s. Effective heat of combustion ΔH_c = 21,500 Btu/lb (given). Combustion efficiency χ = 0.80 (given). Q = χ ṁ ΔH_c.\n\nHeat-release rate?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"4,300 Btu/s"},{"key":"B","text":"21,500 Btu/s"},{"key":"C","text":"3,440 Btu/s"},{"key":"D","text":"1,720 Btu/s"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"Q = 0.80 × 0.20 lb/s × 21,500 Btu/lb = 3,440 Btu/s.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates"],"whyWrong":{"A":"4,300 Btu/s omits χ = 0.80.","B":"21,500 Btu/s is the heat of combustion copied as if it were already the HRR.","D":"1,720 Btu/s used ṁ = 0.10 lb/s or χ = 0.40."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-022","form":"B","number":22,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.C","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A medium t² fire uses α = 0.0117 kW/s² (given). Q = α t². Time to 2.00 MW (2,000 kW)?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"171 s"},{"key":"B","text":"413 s"},{"key":"C","text":"103 s"},{"key":"D","text":"206 s"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"t = √(2,000 / 0.0117) = √170,940 ≈ 413 s.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates"],"whyWrong":{"A":"171 s is 2,000 / 11.7, which treats α as 11.7 and skips the square root.","C":"103 s is √(2,000 / 0.1876), ultrafast.","D":"206 s is √(2,000 / 0.0469), the fast coefficient."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-023","form":"B","number":23,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §3.3 Heat Transfer","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Steady conduction through wood. k = 0.80 Btu·in/h·ft²·°F (given), L = 1.5 in, A = 100 ft², ΔT = 140 °F. q = k A ΔT / L.\n\nHeat-transfer rate, nearest 100 Btu/h?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"11,200 Btu/h"},{"key":"B","text":"75,000 Btu/h"},{"key":"C","text":"1,120 Btu/h"},{"key":"D","text":"7,500 Btu/h"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"q = 0.80 × 100 × 140 / 1.5 = 11,200 / 1.5 = 7,467 Btu/h → 7,500 Btu/h.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.3 Heat Transfer"],"whyWrong":{"A":"11,200 Btu/h forgot to divide by thickness L = 1.5 in.","B":"75,000 Btu/h used L in feet or skipped unit-consistent thickness.","C":"1,120 Btu/h is off by about 10 (dropped a zero / used A = 10 ft²)."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-024","form":"B","number":24,"domain":2,"subdomain":"2.C","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A pallet fire is assigned HRRPUA q″ = 250 kW/m² (given) over a burning area of 4.0 m². Q = q″ A. Heat-release rate?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"4.0 MW"},{"key":"B","text":"0.25 MW"},{"key":"C","text":"1.0 MW"},{"key":"D","text":"250 MW"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"Q = 250 kW/m² × 4.0 m² = 1,000 kW = 1.0 MW.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.2 Fire Growth and Heat Release Rates"],"whyWrong":{"A":"4.0 MW treats 250 as 1,000 kW/m² or multiplies extra by 4.","B":"0.25 MW is q″ without multiplying by area.","D":"250 MW copies the HRRPUA as if it were already MW."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-025","form":"B","number":25,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §7.1 Fundamentals","code":"NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 4","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 12-story airport terminal wants to keep stairwells clear of smoke during a floor-of-origin fire. Which smoke-control approach matches that objective?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Stair pressurization (a smoke-control system type whose design criterion is a pressure difference across the stair door, with door-opening force limits)"},{"key":"B","text":"NFPA 13-2022 Extra Hazard Group 2 sprinklers in the stair in lieu of pressurization"},{"key":"C","text":"Only natural roof vents over the guest-room corridor, with no fan"},{"key":"D","text":"A clean-agent total-flood of the stair"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §7.1 and NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 4: stair pressurization is a listed smoke-control system type. Design criteria are ΔP and door-opening force, not sprinkler density or clean agent.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §7.1 Fundamentals","NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 4"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Sprinkler occupancy hazard does not replace stair pressurization for a keep-the-stair-clear objective.","C":"Natural vents may serve an atrium smoke-fill strategy; they do not pressurize a stair.","D":"Clean agent is a special-hazard suppression agent, not a stair smoke-control method."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-026","form":"B","number":26,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.A","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §7.1 Fundamentals","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An atrium smoke-exhaust design criterion is “maintain the smoke layer interface at least 3.0 m above the highest occupied walking surface.” Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"That is a layer-height design criterion; exhaust is then sized from plume mass flow at that interface (plus any other NFPA 92-2024 prescribed factors in the chosen method)"},{"key":"B","text":"NFPA 92-2024 does not permit mechanical exhaust for atria"},{"key":"C","text":"Once layer height is stated, makeup air may be a high-velocity jet into the layer"},{"key":"D","text":"Layer height is interchangeable with a 0.10 gpm/ft² sprinkler density"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §7.1 / NFPA 92-2024: atrium exhaust methods use a design interface height and plume mass flow at that height.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §7.1 Fundamentals","NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 5"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Mechanical exhaust is a standard NFPA 92-2024 atrium method.","C":"High-velocity makeup into the layer mixes the void and can invalidate the two-layer assumption.","D":"Sprinkler density is not an atrium layer-height criterion."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-027","form":"B","number":27,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §3.4 Plumes and Flames","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Axisymmetric plume mass flow (handbook form given): ṁ = 0.071 Q_c^{1/3} z^{5/3} + 0.0018 Q_c  (kg/s, kW, m) for z above the limiting elevation. Q_c = 2,100 kW, z = 6.0 m.\n\nQ_c^{1/3} ≈ 12.8, z^{5/3} ≈ 19.7. Mass flow, nearest 1 kg/s?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"18 kg/s"},{"key":"B","text":"4 kg/s"},{"key":"C","text":"252 kg/s"},{"key":"D","text":"22 kg/s"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"0.071 × 12.8 × 19.7 ≈ 17.9; 0.0018 × 2,100 ≈ 3.8; sum ≈ 21.7 kg/s → 22 kg/s.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §3.4 Plumes and Flames","NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 5"],"whyWrong":{"A":"18 kg/s is the first term only (forgot +0.0018 Q_c).","B":"4 kg/s is the second term only.","C":"252 kg/s dropped the 0.071 coefficient or mixed units on z."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-028","form":"B","number":28,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §7.1 Fundamentals","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Atrium exhaust is to remove 22,000 cfm of smoke. Makeup air is supplied at the floor. NFPA 92-2024 makeup-air velocity limit given for this item: 200 fpm through the openings so the layer is not destroyed.\n\nMinimum makeup-air opening area?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"110 ft²"},{"key":"B","text":"200 ft²"},{"key":"C","text":"22 ft²"},{"key":"D","text":"11 ft²"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"Q = V A → A = 22,000 cfm / 200 fpm = 110 ft².","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §7.1 Fundamentals","NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 4"],"whyWrong":{"B":"200 ft² inverted the arithmetic (200×22,000 / something).","C":"22 ft² used 1,000 fpm as the limit.","D":"11 ft² is half the opening (used 400 fpm)."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-029","form":"B","number":29,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §7.3 Airflow","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Stair door 0.90 m wide × 2.10 m high. Pressure difference across the door is 50 Pa (given). Handbook door-opening force family uses F = K A ΔP + F_dc  with K A ΔP dominating. Using F_ΔP = A ΔP (N, m², Pa) as the pressure component given for this item, pressure component of opening force?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"9.5 N"},{"key":"B","text":"95 N"},{"key":"C","text":"50 N"},{"key":"D","text":"189 N"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"A = 0.90 × 2.10 = 1.89 m². F = 1.89 × 50 = 94.5 N → 95 N. (Closer door-closer terms would add; the item asks the pressure component.)","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §7.3 Airflow","NFPA 92-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"9.5 N is off by 10.","C":"50 N copied ΔP as if it were already newtons.","D":"189 N used A = 3.78 m² (both sides) or ΔP = 100 Pa."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-030","form":"B","number":30,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §7.3 Airflow","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A smoke-control fan is specified at 12,000 cfm against 1.0 in. w.g. Which statement about using a standard HVAC supply fan curve is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"The smoke-control fan must be listed/capable for the elevated temperature and pressure in the design; a comfort-cooling fan curve at 70 °F is not automatically a smoke-exhaust rating"},{"key":"B","text":"Fan temperature rating is not a smoke-control issue"},{"key":"C","text":"NFPA 13-2022 density tables set the fan cfm"},{"key":"D","text":"Any 12,000 cfm toilet-exhaust fan is acceptable if the cfm matches"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §7 and NFPA 92-2024: smoke-control equipment has temperature and listing constraints. Matching cfm on a comfort fan is not sufficient.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §7.1 Fundamentals","NFPA 92-2024"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Elevated temperature is a first-order selection issue for exhaust fans in smoke.","C":"Sprinkler densities do not size smoke-control fans.","D":"Toilet exhaust is not a smoke-control listing."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-031","form":"B","number":31,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §7.1 Fundamentals","code":"NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 8","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Which activity belongs in NFPA 92-2024 commissioning of a stair-pressurization system rather than in a one-time air-balance of the guest-room HVAC?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Disable all fans for the test so the stair is passive"},{"key":"B","text":"Set VAV boxes to occupied-mode minimums only"},{"key":"C","text":"Commissioning is NFPA 25 and is not on the 2027 exam"},{"key":"D","text":"Measure stair ΔP across typical doors in the design fire mode (fans in smoke-control sequence) and confirm door-opening force is within the design limit"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 8 testing/commissioning: verify the smoke-control sequence, ΔP, and door-opening force. NFPA 25 is not a 2027 listed exam standard; 92 commissioning remains in-scope.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 8"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Disabling fans does not test pressurization.","B":"Occupied VAV minima are HVAC balance, not the smoke-control mode test.","C":"NFPA 25 was removed from the 2027 list; NFPA 92-2024 commissioning is still a listed-standard topic."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-032","form":"B","number":32,"domain":3,"subdomain":"3.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §7.1 Fundamentals","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"During an integrated test the fire-alarm signal that should start atrium exhaust does not start the fans. Which is the correct first troubleshooting focus for a smoke-control commissioning issue?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Add a clean-agent system over the lobby"},{"key":"B","text":"Confirm the fire-alarm control unit sequence of operations actually releases the smoke-control interface (relay/module) and that the fan controller is in the smoke-control mode, not occupied HVAC"},{"key":"C","text":"Increase the sprinkler density one occupancy class"},{"key":"D","text":"Ignore it if the fans run in HAND at the starter"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 92-2024 integrated testing: the automatic sequence from detection/alarm to fan start is the commissioning item. HAND at the starter only proves the motor, not the sequence.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 92-2024 Ch. 8","HB §6.1 General Information"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Clean agent is a different system family.","C":"Sprinkler density will not start a fan.","D":"HAND bypasses the very interface being commissioned."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-033","form":"B","number":33,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §6.1 General Information","code":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 23 / Ch. 24","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A high-rise with an occupant-notification and firefighter telephone/elevator communication need is being specified. Which system selection matches that combination?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"An emergency communications system (ECS) / fire-alarm emergency communication features under NFPA 72-2022, not a conventional four-wire zoned panel with horns only"},{"key":"B","text":"A conventional non-addressable zone panel with 6 in. bells only, because bells are always louder"},{"key":"C","text":"ERCES (emergency responder communication enhancement) as a substitute for occupant notification"},{"key":"D","text":"A clean-agent releasing panel with no occupant notification"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 72-2022: high-rise occupant notification plus firefighter communications are ECS / emergency-communication features. ERCES is in-building responder radio enhancement, not occupant notification. Releasing-only panels are not occupant ECS.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 24","HB §6.1 General Information"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Bells-only conventional zoning does not provide ECS/firefighter communication features.","C":"ERCES supports responder radio; it does not replace occupant notification.","D":"A releasing panel without occupant notification misses the stated need."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-034","form":"B","number":34,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §6.1 General Information","code":"NFPA 72-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A paint-mix room will have a deluge system released by flame detectors. Which fire-alarm system selection is appropriate?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Mass-notification only, with no releasing circuit"},{"key":"B","text":"A releasing fire-alarm control unit listed for releasing service, with the flame detectors as releasing inputs and a documented sequence of operations"},{"key":"C","text":"ERCES in the paint-mix room instead of detection"},{"key":"D","text":"A household smoke-alarm interconnect"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 72-2022 releasing service: the FACU must be listed for releasing, with supervised releasing circuits and a defined sequence. Household alarms, MNS-only, and ERCES do not release deluge.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022","HB §6.1 General Information"],"whyWrong":{"A":"MNS does not open a deluge valve.","C":"ERCES is responder radio, not flame-detector releasing.","D":"Household alarms are not releasing service."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-035","form":"B","number":35,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §6.4 Heat Detector Spacing","code":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 17","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Spot-type heat detectors listed for 50 ft spacing are installed on a smooth 10 ft ceiling. NFPA 72-2022 smooth-ceiling spacing (given): listed spacing may be used; do not exceed the listed spacing. Which layout is consistent?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Heat-detector spacing is taken from NFPA 13-2022 sprinkler spacing tables"},{"key":"B","text":"One detector per floor regardless of area"},{"key":"C","text":"Space at 70 ft because 50 × 1.4 ≈ 70"},{"key":"D","text":"Space detectors at not more than 50 ft, with no detector more than 0.7 × listed spacing from a wall (35 ft) on a smooth ceiling"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 17: listed spacing is a maximum on a smooth ceiling; wall distance is 0.7S. You do not stretch beyond listed spacing by 1.4, and you do not use sprinkler spacing tables.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 17","HB §6.4 Heat Detector Spacing"],"whyWrong":{"A":"NFPA 13 sprinkler spacing is a different standard and purpose.","B":"One detector per floor ignores area and spacing.","C":"1.4 × listed spacing exceeds the listing. 0.7S is the wall distance, not a license to increase S."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-036","form":"B","number":36,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.B","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §6.1 General Information","code":"NFPA 72-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A sequence of operations for a sprinklered classroom wing: smoke detector in the elevator lobby shall recall elevators; waterflow shall notify occupants. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Any alarm input should recall elevators, including a duct detector on a remote AHU"},{"key":"B","text":"The matrix must show waterflow → occupant notification, and lobby smoke → elevator recall, as separate input/output pairs; do not make waterflow the elevator-recall input unless that is the documented design"},{"key":"C","text":"Sequence of operations is optional if the panel is addressable"},{"key":"D","text":"Elevator recall is an NFPA 13-2022 sprinkler-density issue"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 72-2022: sequence of operations maps specific inputs to outputs. Elevator recall is typically lobby smoke (and other 72-required inputs), not every alarm and not waterflow by default.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022","HB §6.1 General Information"],"whyWrong":{"A":"A remote duct detector is not automatically an elevator-lobby recall input.","C":"Addressable hardware does not waive a sequence matrix.","D":"Recall is an alarm/elevator interface, not a sprinkler density."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-037","form":"B","number":37,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §6.8 Voltage Drop Calculation","code":"NFPA 72-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A NAC is loaded to 1.8 A. The panel can supply 3.0 A per circuit. Voltage drop on the circuit at 1.8 A is 1.9 V on a 24 V nominal circuit (calculated). End-of-line voltage is 22.1 V. Devices are listed to operate at 16–33 V. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Voltage drop is always unacceptable if it is not zero"},{"key":"B","text":"The circuit current is within the 3.0 A supply rating and 22.1 V is above the 16 V device minimum, so the NAC as calculated is acceptable from a voltage-drop/current standpoint"},{"key":"C","text":"The circuit must be loaded to 3.0 A to be valid"},{"key":"D","text":"NAC voltage drop is taken from NFPA 20-2022 pump tables"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §6.8 and NFPA 72-2022: stay within power-supply current rating and keep end-of-line voltage above the device listing minimum.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §6.8 Voltage Drop Calculation","NFPA 72-2022"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Some drop is expected. The test is remaining voltage vs listing, not zero drop.","C":"You must not exceed the supply rating; you are not required to load it to 100%.","D":"NFPA 20 pump tables are unrelated to NAC voltage drop."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-038","form":"B","number":38,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §6.8 Voltage Drop Calculation","code":"NFPA 72-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Copper NAC wire: 0.003 Ω/ft (given), one-way length 250 ft, current 1.50 A. Round-trip resistance = 2 × 250 × 0.003. Voltage drop = I R.\n\nVoltage drop?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"1.13 V"},{"key":"B","text":"0.003 V"},{"key":"C","text":"2.25 V"},{"key":"D","text":"4.50 V"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"R = 2 × 250 × 0.003 = 1.50 Ω. Vd = 1.50 A × 1.50 Ω = 2.25 V.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §6.8 Voltage Drop Calculation"],"whyWrong":{"A":"1.13 V used one-way resistance only (forgot the return path).","B":"0.003 V copied the unit resistance.","D":"4.50 V doubled the current or the round trip twice."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-039","form":"B","number":39,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §6.1 General Information","code":"NFPA 72-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Battery standby 24 h at 0.45 A plus 5 min (0.083 h) alarm at 2.40 A. Required capacity = I_s t_s + I_a t_a. A 1.20 aging factor is given for this item.\n\nMinimum battery capacity?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"2.4 Ah"},{"key":"B","text":"32.4 Ah"},{"key":"C","text":"13.2 Ah"},{"key":"D","text":"10.8 Ah"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"Standby = 0.45 × 24 = 10.80 Ah. Alarm = 2.40 × 0.083 ≈ 0.20 Ah. Sum = 11.00 Ah × 1.20 = 13.2 Ah.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022","HB §6.1 General Information"],"whyWrong":{"A":"2.4 Ah is the alarm current copied as amp-hours.","B":"32.4 Ah used 24 h of alarm current (2.4 × 24 × 0. something) or 0.45×24×3.","D":"10.8 Ah is standby only (no alarm, no aging factor)."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-040","form":"B","number":40,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.D","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §6.6 Audibility Design","code":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 18","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Ambient in an classroom is 50 dBA. NFPA 72-2022 public-mode requirement given: the sound level shall be at least 15 dB above average ambient. Required sound level at the pillow/occupant location?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"15 dBA"},{"key":"B","text":"55 dBA"},{"key":"C","text":"85 dBA"},{"key":"D","text":"70 dBA"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"55 + 15 = 70 dBA. (72 also has a 5 dB above maximum-duration ambient rule; this item uses the 15 dB-above-average criterion given in the stem.)","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §6.6 Audibility Design","NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 18"],"whyWrong":{"A":"15 dBA is the margin copied as if it were the required level.","B":"55 dBA is the ambient, not 15 dB above it.","C":"85 dBA used +30 dB."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-041","form":"B","number":41,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.D","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §6.2 Heat Detector RTI","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A heat detector has RTI = 100 m^{1/2}s^{1/2} (given). Ceiling-jet velocity u = 1.0 m/s and temperature rise ΔT_g = 40 K at the detector (given). The handbook time-constant form τ = RTI / √u. τ?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"10 s"},{"key":"B","text":"1 s"},{"key":"C","text":"100 s"},{"key":"D","text":"40 s"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"√u = 1.0. τ = 100 / 1 = 100 s.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §6.2 Heat Detector RTI"],"whyWrong":{"A":"10 s used RTI = 10 or √u = 10.","B":"1 s treated RTI as 1.","D":"40 s copied ΔT."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-042","form":"B","number":42,"domain":4,"subdomain":"4.B","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §6.1 General Information","code":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 21","edition_lock":"NFPA 72-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Smoke control is to start from the fire-alarm system. Which interface is the correct NFPA 72-2022 concept?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"No interface is allowed; fans may only be started by hand"},{"key":"B","text":"A jumper on the HOA station that is not monitored"},{"key":"C","text":"An emergency control function interface (listed relay/module) driven by the fire-alarm sequence, supervised as required, not an unlabeled thermostat wire into the fan starter"},{"key":"D","text":"A plumber’s flow switch on a domestic water heater"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 21 emergency control functions: listed, supervised interfaces from the FACU to the controlled equipment.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 72-2022 Ch. 21"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Automatic start from alarm is the point of the interface.","B":"An unsupervised HOA jumper is not a 72 emergency-control interface.","D":"Domestic water has nothing to do with smoke-control start."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-043","form":"B","number":43,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 8","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An unheated unheated lumber shed in a −10 °F climate will have overhead sprinklers. Which system type is the appropriate selection?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"No sprinklers; freezing weather is an exception to NFPA 13-2022"},{"key":"B","text":"Dry-pipe (or preaction) with listed dry sprinklers/system components — not a wet-pipe tree of water-filled branch lines in the freezing space"},{"key":"C","text":"A clean-agent total-flood of the dock in lieu of sprinklers"},{"key":"D","text":"Wet-pipe with ordinary-temperature heads, because wet-pipe is always fastest"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 8: water-filled piping is not left in freezing spaces. Dry-pipe or dry-pendent/dry systems are the type selection.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 8","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Climate is not a blanket deletion of sprinklers where they are otherwise required.","C":"Clean agent is not the ordinary protection for an open unheated lumber shed.","D":"Wet-pipe in −10 °F freezes. Speed of operation does not override freeze protection."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-044","form":"B","number":44,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A data hall wants no water on the raised floor until a confirmed fire. Which water-based type matches that objective?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Wet-pipe with open deluge nozzles"},{"key":"B","text":"A standpipe-only Class I system with no ceiling sprinklers, as a substitute for room protection"},{"key":"C","text":"Dry-pipe with no detection, advertised as “preaction”"},{"key":"D","text":"Double-interlock preaction (air in the piping; water admitted only after both a detection event and a sprinkler operation / equivalent listed sequence)"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 13-2022: double-interlock preaction is the type that keeps water out of the pipe until detection and sprinkler operation (listed sequence). Wet deluge is the opposite. Standpipe is not ceiling protection. Dry-pipe without detection is not preaction.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 8","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Open deluge puts water on everything when the valve trips.","B":"Class I standpipe is firefighter hose, not room sprinkler protection.","C":"Dry-pipe without a detection interlock is not double-interlock preaction."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-045","form":"B","number":45,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022 §19.3","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Ordinary Hazard Group 2 density/area (given from NFPA 13-2022 Table 19.2.3.1.1 family): 0.20 gpm/ft² over 1,500 ft². Hose-stream allowance given: 250 gpm. Sprinkler demand at that density/area is 300 gpm at the design-area average density.\n\nTotal water-supply demand at the system riser for density/area plus hose (ignore inside/outside hose split)?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"250 gpm"},{"key":"B","text":"300 gpm"},{"key":"C","text":"550 gpm"},{"key":"D","text":"0.20 gpm"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"300 + 250 = 550 gpm. Density/area sprinkler demand plus the hose-stream allowance.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 §19.3","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"250 gpm is hose only.","B":"300 gpm is sprinklers only — the stem requires adding hose.","D":"0.20 gpm is the density, not a flow."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-046","form":"B","number":46,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022 §19.3","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Light Hazard density 4.1 mm/min over 140 m² (given). Demand flow Q = density × area.\n\nSprinkler demand?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"574 L/min"},{"key":"B","text":"41 L/min"},{"key":"C","text":"4.1 L/min"},{"key":"D","text":"140 L/min"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"4.1 mm/min = 4.1 L/min per m². Q = 4.1 × 140 = 574 L/min.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 §19.3","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"B":"41 L/min dropped a factor of 10 or used 0.41 mm/min.","C":"4.1 L/min is the density copied as a flow.","D":"140 L/min is the area copied as a flow."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-047","form":"B","number":47,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A dry-pipe OH2 system uses a 0.20 gpm/ft² density. The wet-pipe design area would be 1,500 ft². NFPA 13-2022 dry-pipe increase given for this item: increase the design area by 30% without revising the density.\n\nDry-pipe design area?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"1,050 ft²"},{"key":"B","text":"1,950 ft²"},{"key":"C","text":"30 ft²"},{"key":"D","text":"1,500 ft²"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"1,500 × 1.30 = 1,950 ft². Density stays 0.20 gpm/ft².","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"1,050 ft² decreased the area 30%.","C":"30 ft² copied the percentage as an area.","D":"1,500 ft² is the wet-pipe area; dry-pipe gets the 30% increase given in the stem."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-048","form":"B","number":48,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A sprinkler is K = 5.6. Required density produces 22 gpm at the most remote sprinkler. Q = K √P. Pressure required at that sprinkler?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"3.9 psi"},{"key":"B","text":"22 psi"},{"key":"C","text":"5.6 psi"},{"key":"D","text":"15.4 psi"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"√P = Q / K = 22 / 5.6 = 3.93. P = 3.93² ≈ 15.4 psi.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","NFPA 13-2022"],"whyWrong":{"A":"3.9 psi is √P, not P.","B":"22 psi copied the flow as pressure.","C":"5.6 psi copied the K-factor."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-049","form":"B","number":49,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.8 Fire Pumps","code":"NFPA 20-2022 Ch. 4 / Ch. 5","edition_lock":"NFPA 20-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A fire pump is to supply 500 gpm at 90 psi net. Which selection is consistent with NFPA 20-2022?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"A 50 gpm jockey pump as the only pump"},{"key":"B","text":"A listed fire pump rated 500 gpm whose churn, rated, and 150% points meet the net-pressure demand, with a driver and controller listed for fire-pump service"},{"key":"C","text":"A domestic booster set at 90 psi with no listing"},{"key":"D","text":"No pump if the city residual is 20 psi at 500 gpm"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 20-2022: listed fire pump, driver, and controller. Churn/rated/150% curve must cover the demand. A domestic booster or a jockey pump is not the fire pump. 20 psi city residual at 500 gpm does not meet 90 psi net.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 20-2022 Ch. 5","HB §4.8 Fire Pumps"],"whyWrong":{"A":"A jockey pump maintains pressure; it is not the 500 gpm fire pump.","C":"Domestic boosters are not listed fire pumps.","D":"20 psi residual is far below 90 psi net demand."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-050","form":"B","number":50,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022 / NFPA 20-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Sprinkler plus hose demand is 750 gpm. Duration given for this occupancy: 60 min. Tank usable volume = Q × t (ignore extra NFPA 22 allowances beyond the stem).\n\nUsable water volume?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"7,500 gal"},{"key":"B","text":"45,000 gal"},{"key":"C","text":"750 gal"},{"key":"D","text":"60 gal"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"750 gpm × 60 min = 45,000 gal.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","NFPA 13-2022"],"whyWrong":{"A":"7,500 gal used 10 min.","C":"750 gal is one minute of flow.","D":"60 gal copied the duration."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-051","form":"B","number":51,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A wet-pipe riser needs a listed alarm check (or equivalent waterflow alarm) and a drain. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Provide a listed waterflow alarm device and an inspector’s test/main drain arranged so the system can be tested and drained — not a silent check valve with no alarm and no drain"},{"key":"B","text":"The only acceptable valve is a backflow preventer with no trim"},{"key":"C","text":"Valves are selected from NFPA 72-2022 NAC tables"},{"key":"D","text":"Omit the alarm device if the building has smoke detectors"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 13-2022: waterflow alarm and drain/test trim are system components. Smoke detection does not replace waterflow alarm. 72 NAC tables are not valve catalogs.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Backflow may be required by the water purveyor but is not a substitute for alarm-check/waterflow trim.","C":"NAC tables do not pick sprinkler valves.","D":"Smoke detection is not a waterflow alarm."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-052","form":"B","number":52,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.1 Fluid Mechanics","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Hazen–Williams friction (handbook US form given): p_f = 4.52 Q^{1.85} / (C^{1.85} d^{4.87}) psi per foot. For this item the computed p_f is 0.080 psi/ft in 100 ft of 4 in. pipe, C = 120, Q = 500 gpm.\n\nFriction loss in that 100 ft?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"80 psi"},{"key":"B","text":"4.52 psi"},{"key":"C","text":"0.080 psi"},{"key":"D","text":"8.0 psi"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"0.080 psi/ft × 100 ft = 8.0 psi.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §4.1 Fluid Mechanics","HB §4.5 Hydraulic Calculations"],"whyWrong":{"A":"80 psi used 1,000 ft or dropped a decimal.","B":"4.52 psi copied the coefficient.","C":"0.080 psi is the unit loss, not the 100 ft total."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-053","form":"B","number":53,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.D","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"K = 80 (metric K, L/min/bar^{1/2}). Pressure at the sprinkler is 1.00 bar. Q = K √P.\n\nFlow?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"80 L/min"},{"key":"B","text":"80 L/s"},{"key":"C","text":"8 L/min"},{"key":"D","text":"1 L/min"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"√1.00 = 1. Q = 80 × 1 = 80 L/min.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"B":"80 L/s mixed seconds for minutes.","C":"8 L/min used K = 8.","D":"1 L/min copied √P."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-054","form":"B","number":54,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.8 Fire Pumps","code":"NFPA 20-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 20-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"City residual at the pump suction flange is 35 psi at 1,000 gpm. Elevation from suction flange to the highest hose valve is 45 ft (0.433 psi/ft given). Hose-valve required residual is 100 psi. Friction in the discharge piping at 1,000 gpm is 12 psi (given).\n\nNet pump pressure required at 1,000 gpm?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"96 psi"},{"key":"B","text":"100 psi"},{"key":"C","text":"12 psi"},{"key":"D","text":"35 psi"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"Discharge needed at pump = 100 + 12 + 45×0.433 = 100 + 12 + 19.5 = 131.5 psi.\nNet = 131.5 − 35 = 96.5 psi → 96 psi.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §4.8 Fire Pumps","NFPA 20-2022"],"whyWrong":{"B":"100 psi is the hose-valve residual only.","C":"12 psi is friction only.","D":"35 psi is suction residual, not net pump pressure."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-055","form":"B","number":55,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §4.5 Hydraulic Calculations","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 4 in. schedule 10 sprinkler main weighs 10 lb/ft filled (given). Hanger spacing 12 ft (given). Load per hanger from pipe weight?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"40 lb"},{"key":"B","text":"12 lb"},{"key":"C","text":"120 lb"},{"key":"D","text":"10 lb"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"10 lb/ft × 12 ft = 120 lb. (Seismic/other loads would add; the item asks pipe-weight load.)","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022","HB §4.5 Hydraulic Calculations"],"whyWrong":{"A":"40 lb used 4 in. as 4 ft of spacing.","B":"12 lb copied the spacing.","D":"10 lb is the unit weight, not the span load."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-056","form":"B","number":56,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §2.4 Fire Protection Analysis—Information Sources","code":"NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 20+","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Cartoned unexpanded plastic, 25 ft storage, 32 ft ceiling. Which design path is the correct first screen?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"NFPA 2001-2022 clean-agent concentration as the storage-sprinkler density"},{"key":"B","text":"Atrium smoke-fill as the sprinkler design method"},{"key":"C","text":"Light Hazard 0.10/1500 because the building is an “classroom wing warehouse” on the architectural title sheet"},{"key":"D","text":"NFPA 13-2022 storage chapters / ESFR or CMDA criteria for that commodity, height, and clearance — not an OH2 0.20/1500 occupancy-hazard density"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 13-2022: storage commodity and height drive ESFR/CMDA/in-rack criteria. Occupancy-hazard OH/LH tables are not a plastic-storage density.","miss_type":"handbook-search","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 20","HB §2.4 Fire Protection Analysis—Information Sources"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Clean-agent design concentration is not a sprinkler density.","B":"Smoke-fill does not size storage sprinklers.","C":"Title-sheet occupancy is not a commodity class."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-057","form":"B","number":57,"domain":5,"subdomain":"5.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems","code":"NFPA 13-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 13-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A freezer at −20 °F is adjacent to a +65 °F wet-pipe warehouse. Dry pendent sprinklers will penetrate the freezer ceiling from a wet main in the warm space. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Run wet branch lines in the freezer if heads are extra-high temperature"},{"key":"B","text":"Use listed dry pendent sprinklers of adequate exposed length, with the wet piping kept in the heated space — do not run water-filled branch lines in the freezer"},{"key":"C","text":"Omit sprinklers in freezers as a 13 exception for all storage"},{"key":"D","text":"Protect the freezer with stair pressurization"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 13-2022: dry pendents from a wet main in a heated space are the usual freeze detail. Water-filled branches in −20 °F freeze regardless of temperature rating of the bulb.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 13-2022 Ch. 8","HB §4.4 Fire Sprinkler Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Bulb temperature rating does not keep water from freezing in the pipe.","C":"There is no blanket “no sprinklers in freezers” exception for all storage.","D":"Stair pressurization is smoke control, not freezer protection."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-058","form":"B","number":58,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §5.5 Clean Agents","code":"NFPA 2001-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 2001-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A CNC electronics cell in a closed 4,000 ft³ room, occupied, with sensitive electronics. Which special-hazard selection is the best first match?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"A wet-chemical plenum fryer system"},{"key":"B","text":"A listed clean-agent total-flood system (NFPA 2001-2022) designed to the agent’s design concentration, with enclosure integrity — not a dry-chemical restaurant system and not “omit suppression because of electronics”"},{"key":"C","text":"NFPA 13-2022 Extra Hazard Group 2 as a clean-agent substitute without an enclosure"},{"key":"D","text":"Portable Class A water extinguishers as the only protection"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §5.5 and NFPA 2001-2022: occupied electronic occupancies are a classic clean-agent total-flood application. Wet chemical is cooking. Water extinguishers are not a total-flood design. EH2 water is a different system family and still needs a water design, not a “clean-agent substitute.”","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 2001-2022 Ch. 5","HB §5.5 Clean Agents"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Wet chemical is for cooking appliances, not a printer farm.","C":"EH2 is a water density, not a clean-agent design concentration.","D":"Portables are not a total-flood special-hazard system."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-059","form":"B","number":59,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §5.1 Foam Agents","code":"NFPA 11-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 11-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 50 ft diameter atmospheric tank of hydrocarbon fuel needs fixed fire protection on the liquid surface. Which agent family is the appropriate selection?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Stair pressurization of the tank"},{"key":"B","text":"CO₂ total-flood keyed only to NFPA 12 as the 2027 listed standard"},{"key":"C","text":"Low-expansion foam (NFPA 11-2024) applied at the listed application rate for the fuel and discharge device — not a clean-agent total-flood of the tank farm yard"},{"key":"D","text":"HFC clean-agent flooding of the diked area as if it were a closed 4,000 ft³ room"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 11-2024 / HB §5.1–5.2: hydrocarbon tank surfaces are a low-expansion foam application. Clean-agent total-flood needs a closed enclosure. NFPA 12 is not a 2027 listed exam standard.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 11-2024","HB §5.2 Low-Expansion Foam Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Stair pressurization is smoke control.","B":"NFPA 12 is not on the 2027 listed-standard sheet.","D":"Clean-agent total-flood is for closed occupiable/equipment rooms, not an open tank surface."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-060","form":"B","number":60,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.B","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §5.5 Clean Agents","code":"NFPA 2001-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 2001-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A clean-agent enclosure fails a fan test (equivalent leakage area too high). Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Enclosure integrity is only an NFPA 25 ITM item and is out of scope"},{"key":"B","text":"Leakage always helps by providing makeup air, so fail the fan test on purpose"},{"key":"C","text":"Switch the scoring key to NFPA 13 Light Hazard"},{"key":"D","text":"Hold time / design concentration cannot be credited until leakage is reduced or make-up agent / pressure-relief is redesigned; do not accept the concentration calculation that assumed a tight box"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 2001-2022: enclosure integrity is a design criterion. A failed fan test means the hold-time assumption is invalid.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 2001-2022","HB §5.5 Clean Agents"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Integrity is a 2001 design/acceptance issue, not a retired NFPA 25-only topic.","B":"Leakage dumps agent; it is not a makeup-air feature for total-flood.","C":"Light Hazard water does not fix a leaking clean-agent enclosure."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-061","form":"B","number":61,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §5.5 Clean Agents","code":"NFPA 2001-2022 Ch. 5","edition_lock":"NFPA 2001-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Clean-agent design concentration is 8.0% by volume (given). Enclosure net volume 4,000 ft³. Flooding factor given for this item: 0.050 lb/ft³ at that concentration.\n\nAgent quantity W = flooding factor × V?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"200 lb"},{"key":"B","text":"8 lb"},{"key":"C","text":"4,000 lb"},{"key":"D","text":"0.050 lb"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"W = 0.050 lb/ft³ × 4,000 ft³ = 200 lb.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §5.5 Clean Agents","NFPA 2001-2022 Ch. 5"],"whyWrong":{"B":"8 lb copied the 8% concentration as pounds.","C":"4,000 lb copied the volume.","D":"0.050 lb copied the flooding factor."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-062","form":"B","number":62,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.D","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §5.5 Clean Agents","code":"NFPA 2001-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 2001-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Net volume 80 m³. Flooding factor 0.80 kg/m³ (given). Agent mass?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"80 kg"},{"key":"B","text":"8 kg"},{"key":"C","text":"64 kg"},{"key":"D","text":"0.80 kg"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"0.80 × 80 = 64 kg.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §5.5 Clean Agents"],"whyWrong":{"A":"80 kg used a factor of 1.0.","B":"8 kg dropped a factor of 8.","D":"0.80 kg copied the factor."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-063","form":"B","number":63,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.D","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §8.1 Flammability","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A vapor is 1.6% by volume in air. LFL is 2.0% (given). The mixture is at 80% of the LFL. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"LFL is a sprinkler density"},{"key":"B","text":"1.6 / 2.0 = 0.80, so the mixture is at 80% of LFL — below LFL, not yet in the flammable range, but above a typical 25% LFL gas-detection action point used in many explosion-prevention schemes"},{"key":"C","text":"Any concentration below 100% LFL is indistinguishable from 0%"},{"key":"D","text":"The mixture is above UFL and cannot burn"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §8.1: flammable range is LFL to UFL. 80% of LFL is still below LFL. Many detection/ventilation setpoints (including 855/69-style 25% LFL) are well below this.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §8.1 Flammability"],"whyWrong":{"A":"LFL is a fuel-air concentration, not a gpm/ft².","C":"25% LFL vs 80% LFL vs 100% LFL are different control points.","D":"UFL is the upper limit, not relevant to 1.6% vs 2.0% LFL."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-064","form":"B","number":64,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §5.2 Low-Expansion Foam Systems","code":"NFPA 11-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 11-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 2,000 ft² hydrocarbon spill area. Application rate given: 0.10 gpm/ft² of foam solution. Foam-solution demand?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"0.10 gpm"},{"key":"B","text":"20 gpm"},{"key":"C","text":"2,000 gpm"},{"key":"D","text":"200 gpm"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"0.10 × 2,000 = 200 gpm foam solution.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 11-2024","HB §5.2 Low-Expansion Foam Systems"],"whyWrong":{"A":"0.10 gpm is the rate, not the demand.","B":"20 gpm used 200 ft².","C":"2,000 gpm used 1.0 gpm/ft²."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-065","form":"B","number":65,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §5.5 Clean Agents","code":"NFPA 2001-2022","edition_lock":"NFPA 2001-2022","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Clean-agent nozzles must cover a 12 ft high enclosure. The listed nozzle has a maximum height of 10 ft. Which component decision is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"One nozzle at the floor aimed up is always equivalent"},{"key":"B","text":"Replace all nozzles with sprinkler uprights"},{"key":"C","text":"Use additional listed nozzles / a listed two-level arrangement so no volume is beyond the listed nozzle height — do not “turn up the concentration 20%” as a substitute for listing"},{"key":"D","text":"Nozzle listing height is only an NFPA 13-2022 storage-height rule"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 2001-2022: discharge devices are used within their listings. Extra concentration does not extend a 10 ft listing to 12 ft.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["NFPA 2001-2022","HB §5.5 Clean Agents"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Floor-mounted aiming is not a listed substitute unless the listing says so.","B":"Sprinkler uprights are water devices.","D":"Nozzle height listing is a 2001 discharge-device limit, not a 13 storage height."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-066","form":"B","number":66,"domain":6,"subdomain":"6.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §3.5 Flammable and Combustible Liquids Fires","code":"NFPA 30-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 30-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An ignitable-liquid process room will have a spill on the floor. Besides suppression, which special-hazard floor feature is in-scope on the 2027 list?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Carpet tile to slow the spill"},{"key":"B","text":"NFPA 12 CO₂ as the only 2027 listed option"},{"key":"C","text":"An ignitable-liquid drainage floor assembly / listed drainage and containment strategy consistent with NFPA 30-2024 process-area intent — not “slope to a hub drain with no interceptor and call it done” if the liquid can reach ignition sources"},{"key":"D","text":"Stair pressurization of the process room"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 30-2024 and the 2027 special-hazard list explicitly include ignitable-liquid drainage floor assemblies. Carpet is not a drainage design. NFPA 12 is not listed for 2027.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 30-2024","HB §3.5 Flammable and Combustible Liquids Fires"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Carpet is a fuel, not a drainage system.","B":"NFPA 12 is not a 2027 listed standard.","D":"Stair pressurization is smoke control."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-067","form":"B","number":67,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A wall assembly is described as “2-hour fire-resistance-rated, tested to ASTM E119 / UL 263.” Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"The hourly rating is from a standard fire-resistance test (time-temperature curve, hose stream as applicable) — it is not a sprinkler density and not a clean-agent hold time"},{"key":"B","text":"ASTM E119 is not used for walls"},{"key":"C","text":"Fire-resistance ratings are taken from NFPA 13-2022 occupancy-hazard tables"},{"key":"D","text":"2-hour means the wall survives a 2,000 °F isothermal furnace indefinitely"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1: fire-resistance ratings come from standard tests (E119/UL 263 family), not from sprinkler tables or agent hold time.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"B":"E119/UL 263 is the usual wall/floor/column test family.","C":"Occupancy-hazard tables are water densities.","D":"E119 uses a standard time-temperature curve, not an isothermal 2,000 °F hold."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-068","form":"B","number":68,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.A","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An existing gypsum assembly is opened and found to have missing layers versus the listed design. Which assessment is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Paint color determines the rating"},{"key":"B","text":"Missing layers are always acceptable if sprinklers are present"},{"key":"C","text":"Ratings only apply to steel"},{"key":"D","text":"The as-built assembly is not the listed rating until repaired to the tested design (or a listed equivalent); do not keep advertising “2-hour” based on the drawing note alone"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1: the rating follows the tested construction. Missing gypsum layers break the listing.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Paint is not the hourly rating.","B":"Sprinklers may be a separate protection feature; they do not automatically restore a missing-layer wall rating.","C":"Gypsum, masonry, and timber assemblies are also rated."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-069","form":"B","number":69,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024 Ch. 8","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 2-hour fire barrier must terminate at a rated floor/roof or an equivalently rated assembly. The wall stops 18 in. below an unrated roof deck with no listed continuation. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Compartmentation is only a sprinkler-density issue"},{"key":"B","text":"18 in. of unrated void is always permitted as a smoke vent"},{"key":"C","text":"The barrier as built does not provide a complete 2-hour compartment; continue the rating to the deck with a listed head-of-wall joint or a rated roof assembly"},{"key":"D","text":"IBC 707 is the 2027 scoring key"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1 and NFPA 101-2024 Ch. 8: barriers must be complete. An 18 in. unrated gap is a failure of compartmentation. IBC is not a supplied 2027 exam standard.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General","NFPA 101-2024 Ch. 8"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Compartmentation is a passive rating/continuity issue.","B":"An unrated gap is a leak, not a designed smoke vent.","D":"Score to NFPA 101-2024 / handbook, not IBC."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-070","form":"B","number":70,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §10.5 Remoteness of Exits","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A rectangular compartment is 30 m × 20 m. Two exits are placed at adjacent corners on the 30 m wall (0 m and 30 m along that wall). Diagonal of the space is 36 m. Remoteness “one-half the diagonal” (given) = 18 m. Separation of the two exits along the wall is 30 m. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"One exit is enough if the diagonal exceeds 20 m"},{"key":"B","text":"30 m ≥ 18 m, so the two exits meet a ½-diagonal remoteness check measured along the line of travel in this simple rectangle (assuming no intervening walls)"},{"key":"C","text":"Exits must be 36 m apart because that is the full diagonal"},{"key":"D","text":"Remoteness is a sprinkler K-factor"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §10.5 / NFPA 101-2024: ½ diagonal = 18 m. 30 m separation ≥ 18 m.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §10.5 Remoteness of Exits","NFPA 101-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Number of exits is a different 101 rule; diagonal does not waive a second exit when 101 requires two.","C":"The criterion given is one-half the diagonal, not the full diagonal.","D":"Remoteness is an exit-separation rule."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-071","form":"B","number":71,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.C","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §9.2 Steel","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An unprotected steel W-shape has W/D = 0.80 lb/ft·in (given). The handbook correlation family uses higher W/D as more fire mass per heated perimeter (more thermal inertia). Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Increasing W/D (heavier section relative to heated perimeter) generally increases the unprotected fire-resistance compared with a skinny, low-W/D section — spray/board protection thickness is still from the listing, not from W/D alone"},{"key":"B","text":"All W-shapes have the same unprotected rating regardless of W/D"},{"key":"C","text":"W/D only applies to timber char"},{"key":"D","text":"W/D is a sprinkler density in gpm/ft²"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.2: W/D is the steel-section factor. Protection thickness still comes from tested designs.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.2 Steel"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Unprotected ratings vary strongly with W/D.","C":"Char is a timber topic (HB §9.4).","D":"W/D is lb/ft per inch of heated perimeter, not gpm/ft²."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-072","form":"B","number":72,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.C","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §9.4 Timber/Wood","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Char rate given: 0.60 mm/min. Required residual section after 30 min. Char depth?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"0.60 mm"},{"key":"B","text":"30 mm"},{"key":"C","text":"6 mm"},{"key":"D","text":"18 mm"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"0.60 mm/min × 30 min = 18 mm.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §9.4 Timber/Wood"],"whyWrong":{"A":"0.60 mm is the rate, not the depth.","B":"30 mm copied the time.","C":"6 mm used 10 min."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-073","form":"B","number":73,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"An engineer proposes substituting a 1-hour listed gypsum shaft wall for a 2-hour required shaft because “the building is sprinklered.” Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Sprinklers do not automatically cut a required 2-hour shaft rating to 1 hour unless the applicable listed code path (here NFPA 101-2024 if that is the scoring document) explicitly permits a reduction — do not invent a 50% sprinkler discount"},{"key":"B","text":"Shaft ratings are selected from NFPA 20-2022 pump tables"},{"key":"C","text":"Sprinklers always halve every rating"},{"key":"D","text":"1-hour and 2-hour assemblies are interchangeable if paint is intumescent"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1 / NFPA 101-2024: rating reductions exist only where the code states them. There is no generic “sprinklers cut ratings in half.”","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General","NFPA 101-2024 Ch. 8"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Pump tables do not pick shaft ratings.","C":"There is no universal 50% sprinkler discount on all ratings.","D":"Intumescent paint is not a blanket equivalency to a 2-hour tested shaft wall."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-074","form":"B","number":74,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.D","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 4 in. metallic pipe penetrates a 2-hour fire barrier. Which protection is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"A listed through-penetration firestop system for metallic pipe in that assembly and hourly rating — not unlisted spray foam from a hardware aisle"},{"key":"B","text":"Firestopping is NFPA 13-2022 hanger spacing"},{"key":"C","text":"Leave an annular gap for movement with no firestop"},{"key":"D","text":"Caulk only the finished-paint side"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1 / NFPA 101-2024: penetrations in rated assemblies use listed firestop systems.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General","NFPA 101-2024 Ch. 8"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Hanger spacing is a sprinkler-support rule.","C":"An open annulus is a failed barrier.","D":"One-side decorative caulk is not a listed system."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-075","form":"B","number":75,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.D","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 2-hour fire barrier is crossed by a 24 × 12 in. HVAC duct. Which opening protectives are in family?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"A residential ceiling fan in the duct"},{"key":"B","text":"A listed fire damper (and smoke damper if the barrier is also a smoke barrier) installed in a listed sleeve/combination as required by the assembly listing"},{"key":"C","text":"Dampers are selected from NFPA 2001-2022 flooding factors"},{"key":"D","text":"No damper if the duct is steel"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"HB §9.1 / NFPA 101-2024: ducts through fire barriers need listed fire dampers (and smoke dampers where the wall is a smoke barrier).","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §9.1 General","NFPA 101-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"A ceiling fan is not a fire damper.","C":"Flooding factors are clean-agent quantities.","D":"Steel duct does not waive a damper in a 2-hour fire barrier."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-076","form":"B","number":76,"domain":7,"subdomain":"7.D","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §9.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 1-hour corridor wall requires a 20-minute smoke-and-draft-control door with a closer. The installed door is an unlisted flush wood door with no closer and a 1 in. undercut. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Closers are optional if the building is sprinklered"},{"key":"B","text":"Corridor doors are sized from NFPA 20-2022"},{"key":"C","text":"The opening protective does not meet a 20-minute listed smoke-and-draft corridor-door assembly; provide a listed door, frame, closer, and listed undercut/gasketing as required"},{"key":"D","text":"A 1 in. undercut is desirable for makeup air in all corridors"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 101-2024: corridor opening protectives are listed assemblies with closers and limited clearances. Sprinklers do not delete closers by a generic rule.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 101-2024","HB §9.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Sprinklers are not a blanket closer deletion.","B":"NFPA 20 does not pick corridor doors.","D":"A 1 in. undercut is a smoke path, not a makeup-air design."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-077","form":"B","number":77,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §10.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024 Table 7.3.1.2","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A 6,000 ft² indoor flea-market hall uses an occupant-load factor of 15 ft²/person (given from NFPA 101-2024 Table 7.3.1.2 for this use). Occupant load?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"15 persons"},{"key":"B","text":"90 persons"},{"key":"C","text":"6,000 persons"},{"key":"D","text":"400 persons"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"6,000 / 15 = 400 persons.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 101-2024 Table 7.3.1.2","HB §10.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"A":"15 persons copied the factor.","B":"90 persons used 67 ft²/person (classroom wing factor) by habit.","C":"6,000 persons used 1 ft²/person."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-078","form":"B","number":78,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §10.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Stair capacity given: 0.3 in. of width per person (sprinklered factor given for this item). A 44 in. clear stair. Capacity of that stair?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"44 persons"},{"key":"B","text":"146 persons"},{"key":"C","text":"15 persons"},{"key":"D","text":"440 persons"}],"answer":"B","solution":{"outline":"44 / 0.3 ≈ 146.7 → 146 persons (integer occupants).","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["NFPA 101-2024","HB §10.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"A":"44 persons copied the width.","C":"15 persons used the occupant-load factor.","D":"440 persons used 0.1 in./person."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-079","form":"B","number":79,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.A","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §10.1 General","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Common path of travel measured 23 m. The allowable common path for this occupancy (given) is 20 m unsprinklered and 30 m sprinklered. The building is sprinklered throughout per NFPA 13-2022. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Travel limits are taken from NFPA 20-2022"},{"key":"B","text":"23 m exceeds 20 m, so the building must be unsprinklered"},{"key":"C","text":"23 m ≤ 30 m, so the common path is within the sprinklered limit given in the stem"},{"key":"D","text":"Common path is a hydraulic calculation"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"NFPA 101-2024: the sprinklered common-path limit given is 30 m. 23 m complies. The 20 m limit is the unsprinklered number.","miss_type":"code-edition","repair_loci":["NFPA 101-2024","HB §10.1 General"],"whyWrong":{"A":"NFPA 20 does not set travel distances.","B":"The 20 m limit is the unsprinklered value; the building is sprinklered.","D":"Common path is an egress geometry rule."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-080","form":"B","number":80,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §10.5 Remoteness of Exits","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"A sprinklered room has a diagonal of 80 ft. One-third diagonal remoteness (given for sprinklered) = 27 ft. Two exits are 40 ft apart. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Remoteness does not apply in sprinklered buildings"},{"key":"B","text":"Use ½ diagonal = 40 ft as a minimum and fail 40 ft because it is not greater than 40"},{"key":"C","text":"40 ft ≥ 27 ft, so remoteness meets the 1/3-diagonal sprinklered criterion given"},{"key":"D","text":"Exits must be 80 ft apart"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"Stem gives 1/3 diagonal for sprinklered = 27 ft. 40 ≥ 27. (½ diagonal would also be 40 ft; the stem’s sprinklered criterion is 1/3.)","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §10.5 Remoteness of Exits","NFPA 101-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Sprinklered buildings still have a remoteness rule; the fraction may change.","B":"The stem’s sprinklered criterion is 1/3, not a strict >½ test that fails equality.","D":"Full diagonal is not the remoteness criterion."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-081","form":"B","number":81,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.B","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Detection 30 s, notification 10 s, pre-movement 60 s, travel 180 s (all given). RSET = sum. ASET from tenability is 240 s (given). Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"RSET is a sprinkler density"},{"key":"B","text":"RSET = 180 s because only travel counts"},{"key":"C","text":"ASET > RSET because 240 > 180"},{"key":"D","text":"RSET = 280 s > ASET = 240 s, so the performance comparison fails as given; reduce RSET or increase ASET"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"RSET = 30+10+60+180 = 280 s. 280 > 240, so occupants are still moving after tenability is lost.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement"],"whyWrong":{"A":"RSET is a time, not a density.","B":"Travel is only one RSET term.","C":"Comparing 240 to travel-only 180 ignores detection, notification, and pre-movement."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-082","form":"B","number":82,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.B","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Specific flow given: 24 persons/min per foot of stair width. 44 in. = 3.67 ft. Flow capacity of that stair?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"88 persons/min"},{"key":"B","text":"24 persons/min"},{"key":"C","text":"44 persons/min"},{"key":"D","text":"3.67 persons/min"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"24 × 3.67 ≈ 88 persons/min.","miss_type":"arithmetic","repair_loci":["HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement"],"whyWrong":{"B":"24 is the specific flow, not the stair capacity.","C":"44 copied the width in inches.","D":"3.67 copied the width in feet."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-083","form":"B","number":83,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.C","type":"mcq4","units":"SI","handbook":"HB §10.3 Egress Behavior in Smoke","code":"NFPA 92-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 92-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Tenability is stated as visibility ≥ 10 m and upper-layer temperature ≤ 60 °C at head height. Predicted visibility is 6 m at 90 s while temperature is 45 °C. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Temperature is the only tenability metric"},{"key":"B","text":"Tenability is an NFPA 13-2022 density"},{"key":"C","text":"Tenability has already failed on visibility at 90 s even though temperature is still below 60 °C — ASET is no later than that 90 s mark for this criterion set"},{"key":"D","text":"Visibility of 6 m is better than 10 m"}],"answer":"C","solution":{"outline":"HB §10.3: tenability is a set of limits. The first limit crossed (visibility here) sets ASET.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §10.3 Egress Behavior in Smoke","HB §7.2 Smoke"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Visibility and toxicity can fail before temperature.","B":"Tenability is an occupant-exposure criterion.","D":"6 m is worse (less) visibility than 10 m."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-084","form":"B","number":84,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.C","type":"mcq4","units":"none","handbook":"HB §10.3 Egress Behavior in Smoke","code":null,"edition_lock":null,"cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Occupants in a windowless inner classroom wing delay pre-movement because the first cue is a distant horn, not smoke they can see. Which statement is correct for a performance egress analysis?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Increase the pre-movement (and possibly notification) component of RSET to reflect delayed recognition; do not assume instant travel when the only cue is a remote audible device"},{"key":"B","text":"Only structural fire ratings affect pre-movement"},{"key":"C","text":"Decision-making time is not part of RSET"},{"key":"D","text":"Pre-movement is always 0 s in classroom wings"}],"answer":"A","solution":{"outline":"HB §10.3 / §10.2: recognition and decision are in RSET. A poor cue lengthens pre-movement.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §10.3 Egress Behavior in Smoke","HB §10.2 Evacuation Movement"],"whyWrong":{"B":"Structural ratings do not set pre-movement.","C":"Decision/recognition is a standard RSET term.","D":"classroom wing pre-movement is not identically zero."}}},
{"id":"FPE-2027-B-085","form":"B","number":85,"domain":8,"subdomain":"8.A","type":"mcq4","units":"US","handbook":"HB §10.4 Emergency Lights","code":"NFPA 101-2024","edition_lock":"NFPA 101-2024","cognitive":"apply","time_budget_s":360,"figure":null,"stem":"Emergency illumination along the path of egress is specified as 1.0 footcandle average (given). A designer provides only photoluminescent exit signs and no emergency lighting in a windowless sterile corridor. Which statement is correct?\n\nForm B uses this occupancy and the values printed in the stem. Do not reuse Form A numbers from memory.","choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Photoluminescent signs always replace emergency lighting"},{"key":"B","text":"1.0 footcandle is a sprinkler residual pressure"},{"key":"C","text":"Emergency lighting is an NFPA 20-2022 pump-room-only rule"},{"key":"D","text":"Exit signs are not a substitute for the required emergency illumination of the walking surface; provide emergency lighting to the stated 1.0 fc average (and any minimum given by 101) along the path"}],"answer":"D","solution":{"outline":"HB §10.4 and NFPA 101-2024: path illumination and exit marking are separate. Signs mark the door; they do not light the floor.","miss_type":"concept","repair_loci":["HB §10.4 Emergency Lights","NFPA 101-2024"],"whyWrong":{"A":"Signs are marking, not floor illumination, unless a specific 101 path says otherwise for that occupancy — the stem’s 1.0 fc walking-surface criterion is still unmet.","B":"Footcandle is illuminance, not psi.","C":"Emergency lighting is an egress feature, not only a pump-room rule."}}}
];
